  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:28:25.307Z",
  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nIs an individual required to file an income tax return if they have no income tax due?\n\n### Short Answer\nYes, an individual is still required to file an income tax return even if they have no income tax due. This requirement is established under Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC).\n\n### Accounting Treatment\nFrom an accounting perspective, individuals must prepare and submit an income tax return to comply with tax regulations, regardless of whether they owe taxes. This ensures proper documentation of income and adherence to filing requirements.\n\n### Tax Treatment\nAccording to Section 51 of the NIRC, every Filipino citizen residing in the Philippines, as well as every alien residing in the Philippines, is required to file an income tax return based on their income derived from sources within and outside the Philippines. This includes individuals with no tax due, as the filing obligation is not solely contingent on the amount of tax owed.\n\n### Differences and Reconciliation\nThe distinction lies in the nature of the income and the tax liability. While individuals with no income tax due may not have a tax payment obligation, the requirement to file remains. This is particularly relevant for individuals receiving purely compensation income from a single employer, who may be eligible for substituted filing under Section 51-A of the NIRC, which allows them to forgo filing if their tax has been withheld correctly.\n\n### Practical Note\nIt is essential for individuals to be aware of their filing obligations to avoid penalties for non-compliance. Even if no tax is due, timely filing of the income tax return is crucial to maintain good standing with the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR) and to ensure that all income is properly reported.",
